r's papers. But a will was Margaret Ellison's, and the witness was real, and the truth — Margaret Ellison clung to this — the truth is stubborn in any century.\n\nThe witness lived in a Cornish village's poorest court, old and afraid and tired of being afraid. a will in Margaret Ellison's hands loosened their tongue: yes, they had seen. Yes, they would swear. No, they had never dared."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "It came to a head at the assembly rooms, in front of all a Cornish village: Margaret Ellison stood and spoke, with a will held high and the witness beside them. The gaslight seemed to lean in to listen.\n\nMargaret Ellison stood in the emptying hall with a will and felt not triumph but tiredness, and under it, peace. truth endures. It had cost plenty. It was worth it.\n\nthe mill owner laughed — then didn't. The evidence was a chain, each link forged: a will, the register, the witness, the ledger page the old soldier had risked everything to copy. Link by link, it held."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Restoration', 'text': "truth endures — Margaret Ellison had it carved, finally, on no monument at all, just lived it, in a Cornish village, in every ordinary, extraordinary day.\n\nthe old soldier married the vicar's son — or daughter; a Cornish village gossiped happily either way — and named their first child after Margaret Ellison, who pretended to mind and didn't.\n\na Cornish village changed, the way places do when a bad tooth is pulled: slowly, then all at once. The mill got new owners. The court got a new magistrate. a will got a place of honor — and then, deliberately, a quiet drawer."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Coda: The Drawer', 'text': "a will rests now in Margaret Ellison's drawer, among the bills and the birthday cards. Sometimes Margaret Ellison takes it out, just to hold the weight of it: proof that one person, in a Cornish village, in any age, can tip the scales.\n\nThe gaslight is electric now. The carriages are cars. But a will is still a will, and Margaret Ellison is still Margaret Ellison, and the drawer still opens.\n\nthe old soldier visits with the children, who are told the story as a fairy tale — which, Margaret Ellison reflects, is what all true stories become, if they're lucky."}]}

